1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,964
This is Sam. He looks after the data jobs at a small company.

2
00:00:04,964 --> 00:00:10,840
Every night, an old job exports the company's 40 customer accounts to a CSV file.

3
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:17,833
Sam has rewritten that job. Before he switches over, he has to show the new export matches the old one.

4
00:00:17,833 --> 00:00:26,693
This is the old export. Each account has a region, a status, a balance, and a note.

5
00:00:26,693 --> 00:00:32,600
They do not look the same. Regions are in capitals, and statuses are single letters.

6
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:38,044
So Sam runs Veridelta on the two files, pairing the rows by account ID.

7
00:00:38,044 --> 00:00:43,399
39 accounts differ, and one is missing from the rewrite.

8
00:00:43,399 --> 00:00:47,019
The exit code is 1, so in CI, this run would fail.

9
00:00:47,019 --> 00:00:50,033
But which of these differences are real errors?

10
00:00:50,033 --> 00:00:54,061
Veridelta pairs each row with its match, by the key.

11
00:00:54,061 --> 00:00:58,017
Rules tell it which differences Sam expects, column by column.

12
00:00:58,017 --> 00:01:02,267
Whatever the rules do not explain is reported. Nothing is hidden.

13
00:01:02,267 --> 00:01:05,983
Sam starts from a configuration with no rules at all.

14
00:01:05,983 --> 00:01:11,427
veridelta suggest tries each kind of rule on the columns that differ, without calling a model.

15
00:01:11,427 --> 00:01:20,467
It finds three, each with the rows it explains: letter case in region, rounding in balance, and N/A as an empty note.

16
00:01:20,467 --> 00:01:31,246
Sam keeps all three, and adds one suggest could not guess: the old status words, mapped to the new letters.

17
00:01:31,246 --> 00:01:33,700
Now only two accounts differ.

18
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:41,088
Rules can forgive rounding, case, empty markers, renamed codes, dates, and types.

19
00:01:41,088 --> 00:01:44,732
They cannot do arithmetic, like rounding to a multiple of seven.

20
00:01:44,732 --> 00:01:49,633
And they cannot compare two columns. Rows pair only by their key.

21
00:01:49,633 --> 00:01:59,879
The missing account, number 40, was closed on purpose. Sam writes that down in a baseline file.

22
00:01:59,879 --> 00:02:07,130
Veridelta accepts the closed account. One account still differs.

23
00:02:07,130 --> 00:02:09,200
So the run still fails.

24
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:17,020
The report shows that row side by side. Account 17 is in the south in the old export, and in the east in the rewrite.

25
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:20,900
That is the real bug, one among 40 differences.

26
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:26,056
On every pull request, the GitHub Action runs the same check and comments the result.

27
00:02:26,056 --> 00:02:30,833
Until account 17 is fixed, the check fails, so the bug cannot ship.

28
00:02:30,833 --> 00:02:35,413
Sam fixes the region in his rewrite and runs the check on the new export.

29
00:02:35,413 --> 00:02:37,888
It passes.

30
00:02:37,888 --> 00:02:41,900
The exit code is 0. Sam can switch off the old job.

31
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:45,496
AI agents can run the same check through MCP.

32
00:02:45,496 --> 00:02:48,700
And warehouse tables are compared where they are stored.

33
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:52,536
Veridelta is early, and tested on generated data.

34
00:02:52,536 --> 00:02:54,300
Feedback is welcome.

35
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:59,633
Try it with pip install veridelta.
